mixin () {
  /// Static behavioral documentation for the Canine Courier backend.
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Canine Courier Backend API\n\n## Purpose\nThe backend persists per-player progress for the Canine Courier game: each caller's best score and total lost items collected, plus their preferred control scheme and sound preference. All records are keyed by the caller's principal.\n\n## Authentication\nEvery method is keyed to the caller identity. `getMyStats`, `recordRun`, `getMySettings` and `saveMySettings` all read or write only the calling principal's own record; no method accepts another user's principal. Anonymous callers are permitted at the canister level and simply receive their own (initially empty) record, so a caller that never signed in gets `null` from the read methods.\n\nThe app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n\n## Authorization\nThere is no role gate on the game methods: any caller may read and write its own stats and settings. The authorization mixin is included for platform compatibility and exposes the standard access-control surface; it does not restrict the game endpoints. OQL reads are scoped per user: each signed-in caller sees only its own rows, and the platform controller sees all rows.\n\n## Identity derivation\nRecords are keyed by the raw caller principal (`msg.caller`). A principal derived against a different derivation origin is a different principal and therefore sees a different, empty record.\n\n## Units and encoding\n`bestScore` and `totalItemsCollected` are non-negative `Nat` counts. `controlScheme` is a variant with tags `wasd` and `arrows`. `soundEnabled` is a `Bool`. Read methods return `null` (an optional) when the caller has no stored record yet.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n`getMyStats` and `getMySettings` are query methods and return immediately from replicated state. `recordRun` and `saveMySettings` are update methods and take effect once the message is committed. There is no asynchronous job to poll; a client may re-query after an update to observe the new value.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n`saveMySettings` is idempotent: saving the same settings twice yields the same stored value. `recordRun` is NOT idempotent: each call accumulates `itemsCollected` into `totalItemsCollected`, so retrying the same run double-counts items. `bestScore` is monotonic (only a higher score replaces it), so retrying a run never lowers the best score. Callers should record each finished run exactly once.\n\n## Errors and gotchas\nNo method traps on ordinary input; scores and item counts are plain non-negative integers. The only non-obvious behavior is that `recordRun` accumulates rather than replaces, and that reads return `null` until the first write for that principal.";
  };
};
